comments.body:
Cloning the signing context before randomizing it would skip the second SHA256 self-test.

`secp256k1_context_clone` copies the context, compression function included, and runs no self-test. Done before `secp256k1_context_randomize`, the verify context ends up in the same state as now and shares no blinding. In medians of three bench_bitcoin runs on an Apple M5, ECCContextCreate drops from 169 µs to 89 µs. The parse_der_lax fuzz target builds a context for every input that parses, so it saves the same time per input. Unit tests pass.

```suggestion
/** Create an elliptic curve context that uses our SHA256 implementation */
static secp256k1_context* ECC_Start() {
    secp256k1_context *ctx = secp256k1_context_create(SECP256K1_CONTEXT_NONE);
    assert(ctx != nullptr);

    SHA256AutoDetect();
    secp256k1_context_set_sha256_compression(ctx, SHA256Transform);

    return ctx;
}

ECC_Context::ECC_Context(std::span<const unsigned char> rng_seed32)
{
    assert(g_ecc_context == nullptr);
    assert(rng_seed32.empty() || rng_seed32.size() == 32);
    m_sign_ctx = ECC_Start();
    // Clone before randomizing: the copy keeps SHA256Transform without testing it again,
    // and does not share the blinding of the signing context.
    m_verify_ctx = secp256k1_context_clone(m_sign_ctx);
    assert(m_verify_ctx != nullptr);
    if (!rng_seed32.empty()) {
        // Pass in a random blinding seed to the signing context.
        bool ret = secp256k1_context_randomize(m_sign_ctx, rng_seed32.data());
        assert(ret);
    }
    g_ecc_context = this;
}
```

